poem:
Where war has sharpened his weapons,
Stood frowning there, a cave here, bred
six
Who made the gallows an altar high,
Thy signals, ever and anon she cried;
But would please the weak frail flesh;

And careworn brows forget.
Let me dream
Of captive wind that moans for fields and
seas;
Sickle comes and leaves us stunned and dazed;

With the links and cords of love.
How some lady's go'nter
Boss dat little hall;
Des you take my heart,
To a dead man's brow;

And slavery, with its morning
lay;
They had met in deadly strife;
Men to say "Dis is yo' las'"--
De thun'ers ur Mount Sin-a-i
Comes from your trembling throat;
